    {
      "object_id": "mathia_interpretation_5d00744ce51ba56e57f64d3e9b36fa27ba5907d07c427287c2f06b46eb78db55",
      "object_role": "interpretation",
      "proposed_content": "Selberg's conjectural prime-coefficient second moments and cross-correlations supply an inner-product-like geometry in which primitive Selberg-class functions form the source's stated orthonormal system. This replaces classification by origin with asymptotic coefficient comparison and, if the conjecture holds, yields the listed consequences for Artin holomorphy and uniqueness of primitive factorization. After vertical shifts are admitted, the specified pairing with a shifted zeta function is stated to be equivalent to a prime number theorem for F.",
