    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_213dd1ed96ab12f90413e73f399c2b939d67b4f62fc54e874fbb7e67ee926b0c",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "Euler products provide local prime factors, while the Selberg axioms package such local data together with a Dirichlet series, continuation, a functional equation, and coefficient bounds into a product-stable class with primitive factorization. Selberg's conjectural prime-coefficient orthogonality then links primitives to uniqueness and, after allowing vertical shifts, to a prime number theorem; separate zero-free and density hypotheses grade global zero information that can be posed for an L-function.",
